course: dictionary
lesson: 2571
title: Tricked by a word: delude
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To delude someone means to trick them into believing something that is not true. «Don't delude yourself» means do not fool yourself into believing something false just because you wish it were true.
explain: Look at the clues in Abby's sentence: she tells her gran it is «delulu», short for deluded, to say the washing machine changed more lives than the internet. The claim is false, and deluded tells you the gran would be believing something untrue.
explain: Use the swap test. Put your meaning into the sentence: «Deluded into thinking a made-up story is true» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. Delusion is the noun for the false belief itself.
fact: Delude is a verb: delude, deludes, deluded, deluding.
fact: «Delude» means to trick someone into believing something that is not true.
fact: «Delude yourself» means to believe something untrue because you want it to be true.
fact: «Deluded» means believing false things, and a «delusion» is a false belief.
fact: «Delulu» (Gen Alpha slang) is short for deluded, and is often used jokingly.
exercise: «Don't delude yourself, that lottery ticket is not a sure win.» What does delude yourself mean here? | Ask a friend to check your ticket | Forget where you put the ticket | *Believe something untrue because you want it to be true | Count your money very carefully
exercise: Find and copy one word that means a false belief. | *delusion | sure | true | wise
exercise: «The fake website wanted to ___ people into giving away their passwords.» Choose the word that fits the gap. | delight | dilute | deliver | *delude
test: «He was deluded into thinking the old map showed buried gold.» What does deluded mean? | Hungry for adventure | *Believing something false | Lost in the woods | Very rich from old treasure
test: Which word is the noun for a false belief? | Deluded | Delude | Deluding | *Delusion
exam: Which sentence uses delude correctly? | The rain will delude the garden tomorrow. | The bus will delude the road at noon. | She deluded the sandwich into lunch. | *The magician tried to delude the audience with a clever trick.
